module SampleInchRuler

@ Author = "Benjamin Boeck" @

@ WorkpieceGeometry :"MinEdge":{"X":0.0,"Y":0.0,"Z":-10.0},"MaxEdge":{"X":175.0,"Y":30.0,"Z":0.0}@

# SAMPLE: a real inch ruler - whole inches, 1/2, 1/4, 1/8 and 1/16 - in one command.
#
#   EngraveRuler X=0.4 Y=0.4 Z=0 length=6      6 inch long, starting at X=0.4 inch / Y=0.4 inch
#
# Inch without conversion: AxisSystem::ImperialMeasuring switches the whole control to inch. From then
# on every position, length and feed in the program is in inch (feed in inch/min), and the defaults of
# the library (tick 6 mm, depth 0.1 mm, heights ...) are converted automatically. On an inch control the
# kind of the ruler is Inch by itself. AxisSystem::MetricMeasuring at the end switches back.
#
# How an inch ruler is built: the inch is halved again and again and every level has its own tick length.
#     level 0  whole inch   longest tick, big number      1  2  3 ...
#     level 1  1/2 inch     70 percent of it, small number 1/2
#     level 2  1/4 inch     49 percent, small number       1/4  3/4
#     level 3  1/8 inch     34 percent, no number
#     level 4  1/16 inch    24 percent, no number
# Every level is `factor` (default 0.7) times as long as the one before it. Useful changes:
#     levels=5            continue to 1/32 inch
#     fractionLevel=1     numbers only for the halves (0 = no fractions, 3 = also eighths)
#     tickLength=0.3      a longer longest tick; everything else follows
#
# The program selects the tool and sets Rpm, the feeds and the spindle. Z = 0 is the surface of the part.

using Base
using ScaleEngraver
import AxisSystem

export program Main
    AxisSystem::ImperialMeasuring
    Absolute
    SafeZHeightForWorkpiece = 0.6
    Rpm = 13000
    SetFeedTechnology plunge=8 finishing=30
    Spindle On

    EngraveRuler X=0.4 Y=0.4 Z=0 length=6

    Spindle Off
    AxisSystem::MetricMeasuring
    MoveToParkPosition
endprogram

end